# Fresh upgrade capture patch

`fresh-upgrade-capture.patch` is unapplied. It adds explicit capture preparation in the browser service and registrations without storing a canvas, ImageData or DOM reference in World. No tracked file was changed during this preparation.

## Proposed implementation

- `CaptureLayer.prepare?: () => void` refreshes its owned paint from current model state, without advancing a clock.
- `StageCapture.capture({ refresh: true })` snapshots composition-ordered layers, prepares all still-registered layers before copying any, verifies a base still exists, then copies the same still-owned layers. Registrations created during preparation wait for another capture. Preparation errors propagate rather than silently returning stale pixels.
- Default `capture()` preserves its existing non-preparing behavior, including pause capture. Layer snapshot/removal checks avoid copying disposed owners.
- GameViewport's base layer prepares with `renderer.draw(world, settingsRef.current)`. `renderer.capture` previously only rendered the existing Pixi scene graph; it did not update world/HUD nodes from the latest simulation state.
- MainStage's effects layer prepares by repainting current MainEffects flashes. Its existing flash paint is extracted into a local function shared by this preparation and the normal RAF tail. No effects clocks advance here.
- UpgradeMenu requests `refresh: true` for its one constructor capture. Initial image ownership and retained history remain in the browser-owned menu service.

## Stale-data and coverage audit

GameRenderer.draw updates field/tank/projectile paint, tactical HUD, minimap, life/fire HUD and edit slider from current model state. HudView reads the interface-phase captured readout; refreshing does not run another interface frame. World.onUpgradeMenuOpen is already synchronous before field work, so an immediate preparation/capture there avoids the later field/avatar updates.

MainStage was a second stale layer: its flash canvas was painted only after StageRuntime.advance completed. MainEffects had already advanced before World.Run, so refreshing only Pixi could capture the previous RAF's flash paint. The proposed shared painter fixes that boundary without calling effects.tick twice.

MainBackground capture already calls its view.draw(stage.background) while composing the capture, so it needs no additional prepare callback. It reads the current background model synchronously.

MainStage's CSS shake transform is a transform of the captured root. This patch keeps the existing root-local capture coordinates rather than rasterizing or applying browser CSS transforms. Exact source root/matrix and full capture equivalence remain comparison limits.

GameHud's map/edit controls are transparent and its readouts are screen-reader text. Its pause-hit Icon is visibly painted DOM and is not a StageCapture layer. The refresh patch does not rasterize that browser pause affordance; this existing adapter/capture-coverage limit remains explicit.

No browser pixels or original runtime are verified by the unit canvas port. Exact constructor/frame-script ordering, complete Main capture coverage and original/Adobe pixel equivalence remain unproved.

## Main4 versus World artwork

Original Main.Run case4 skips World.Run (`Main.as532–558`), while the attached display tree still has independent timeline work. An isUpgradeOpen predicate based on the retained menu owner, independent of world.mode, is appropriate. Extracting the existing World.step finally artwork pass into World.advanceArtwork can preserve those clocks without advancing the World listener's hit timer, interface, field combat or effects-engine polling.

Check the menu predicate at entry to the viewport step. If the menu is born during that step, the already-selected original World.Run still completes; do not stop it midway or invoke artwork a second time. Subsequent menu frames can call advanceArtwork once. Keep wall-time callbacks in the existing StageRuntime.elapsed path, and keep MainEffects ticking once from the shared stage. A capture prepare must render only; it must not call either World.step or advanceArtwork.

The retained upgrade service's step runs after the world listener. Its final Destroy restores control after that frame's Main/world turn; gameplay resumes in the next fixed-frame cohort. The separate cohort patch preserves fresh-listener next-frame entry.

## Confirmed background-ownership contradiction

`BTAGameWorld._-7x` unconditionally calls both background `_-KD` resume methods while restoring its saved mode (`BTAGameWorld.as467–475`). PlaceAvatar's first-secondary-help path writes both world modes to1 (`1065–1077`) but does not stop those backgrounds. Therefore an upgrade can close into secondary help with backgrounds resumed.

GameViewport.backgroundState currently infers stop/resume solely from world.mode. After that close, a changed secondary-help mode causes it to stop the backgrounds again, contradicting this source path. Background pause/resume needs its source owner/event latch; mode alone is insufficient. This patch deliberately does not change that ownership or World artwork behavior; parent owns those layer5 integrations.

## Validation

- Seven capture-service tests /24 assertions pass, covering stale/default capture, all-prepares-before-copy order, missing/released base, owner removal, late registration and propagated preparation failure.
- Two focused tests fail against the current capture service because refresh is ignored: stale base paint and missing preparation order. See `before.log`.
- Proposed StageCapture, GameViewport, MainStage, UpgradeMenu and tests typecheck virtually using the real tsconfig options with no emitted files/build state.
- `git apply --check` passes. Parent still needs repository checks and affected browser tests after applying and integrating the World/background changes.

The implementation follows source/setup ordering and inspected tagged Ruffle broadcast contracts. Tagged-source byte matching does not prove loaded/compiled Ruffle provenance, an observed original execution trace, or Adobe behavior.
